  dailyRead: "The honest headline today is a negative result that has now been tested twice. Yesterday the dollar refused to rally on the hottest CPI and PPI pair of the year, and the available explanation was credit: an AI-financing scare pushed the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index down about 4% in a sixth straight down session, CoreWeave bonds to distressed levels and the S&P 500 down 1.11%, and a US-centric credit shock is a dollar-negative risk-off. Overnight that explanation was removed. The Nikkei closed up 2.01% at 65,720 after Thursday's near-5% tumble, the Kospi gained roughly 3% with Samsung up 2.9% and SK Hynix up 2.22%, the DAX is up 0.69% at 25,559, the Euro Stoxx 50 up 0.72% at 6,215.70, US futures are green and copper is up 1.32% at 6.5972 with LME stocks at a six-week low. Risk came back. The dollar did not: DXY is 101.899 at minus 0.09%, down 0.31% on the week, lower against six of seven majors for a second consecutive session, and the ten-year is 5.0890 after falling from 5.19% on Tuesday - so the curve will not confirm the inflation data either. With the FOMC communications blackout beginning Saturday and no CPI before the meeting, the 28.0% centralbank.watch prices for October 28 now has to be resolved by nothing at all. The dollar therefore keeps plus 1 on a December 9 hike that is 89.8% priced, but its verdict moves from STRENGTHENING to MIXED, and that is the most important line in this file: a tightening that is almost fully paid for and still cannot be bought is not strength, it is a position that has already been taken. The two score changes are in the currencies the reversal hit directly. The franc is cut from plus 1 back to 0 one session after the upgrade, and both legs that earned it broke. Yesterday's case was that the franc gained against all seven majors - the first haven test in five weeks the market actually paid for - and that centralbank.watch priced the SNB's December 10 assessment at a 21.9% hike. Today the franc is lower against six of seven majors, with NZD/CHF up 0.55%, AUD/CHF up 0.37% and GBP/CHF up 0.21%, and that December pricing has fallen to 17.9%. Same test, opposite answer, which is what a pure risk proxy does when the risk goes away. Gold is the control and it is worth more than either score: XAU/USD is 4,175.09 and up 0.10% on the day, because gold trades US real rates as well as fear and the ten-year has come down about 10bp this week, while the franc only owns the fear leg. The Canadian dollar is raised from 0 to plus 1, and the reason is precisely the leg this file reported as stalled yesterday. WTI has now gone 90.409, 92.715, 94.810, 94.022 and 97.290 across five readings, up 5.92% on the week with Brent at 105.311, so the stall is over. The physical case behind it got worse rather than better: Kpler has Hormuz exports at 8.5 million barrels a day in the week to October 12, close to 40% below pre-war normal, UKMTO counts more than twenty vessels attacked this month and nine in or near the strait since October 12, two cargo ships sank this week, and an Iranian army chief says the strait stays shut until Iran's rights are respected. Macklem's Halifax threshold - that if oil stays near 100 dollars the Bank would expect inflation to edge up, and that there is a risk in delaying a hike too long - is now effectively met, and the October 28 Monetary Policy Report has to print a crude assumption to replace the 75 dollars by mid-2027 that its last baseline carried. Over the week the Canadian dollar is firmer against six of seven majors. What caps it at plus 1 is labour, still the worst on this board: employment minus 68.3K in September, unemployment 6.5% and the employment rate down to 60.6%. Everything else held, and in two cases holding was the decision rather than the default. Sterling stays plus 1 and keeps STRENGTHENING on a November 5 hike that centralbank.watch prices at 93.6% with three MPC members already voting for it, but the conviction problem named yesterday is now in the data as well as in Bailey's words: he told the Bangkok audience that second-round effects are quite subdued and that businesses are emphasising cost-cutting rather than passing costs on, and UK gas has come back to 211.47 GBp/thm, down 1.02% on the day with its weekly gain halved to 4.40% from 9.88%. His energy conditional is easing from both ends at once. The euro holds plus 1 with its withdrawn support repairing - OAT-Bund back to 138.45bp from 141.74bp, the ECB's December 17 hike now 85.3% priced against a 95.8% hold on October 29 - while the wage series Lagarde says the Council will anticipate still will not cooperate, at 2.44% negotiated and 3.0% whole-economy. The yen holds 0 and it is the most interesting hold here: the US-Japan ten-year gap compressed for a second straight session to 214.80bp from 218.30bp, the JGB rose to 2.9410 while the Nikkei gained 2%, and BoJ December 17 now carries 59.2% for a higher rate. A yen that firms against the dollar, the euro, the franc and the Canadian dollar on a strong risk day is trading its own rate story rather than the carry, and that is new. The Australian dollar holds 0 with its legs pulling against each other: November 3 has collapsed to a 15.0% hike from 25.7% yesterday and better than 80% in September, while copper repaired to plus 1.32% with the LME cash-to-three-month premium at 181 dollars, the highest since late August, and the Yangshan premium at a four-year high. The New Zealand dollar holds minus 1 and was the day's best performer, higher against all seven majors and up 0.51% on the dollar off a seventeen-month low - a squeeze into the October 21 CPI, not a repair. The cleanest trades are where a central-bank divergence is dated and not yet fully in the price: GBP/AUD long on a 93.6% November hike against a 15.0% one, EUR/NZD long into a CPI whose official forecast is already a deceleration, and the newly opened Canadian legs - CAD/CHF and CAD/JPY long, AUD/CAD short - where the oil leg and the haven unwind point the same way.",
