  geopolitics: "The transmission chain has reconnected at the price end while continuing to deteriorate at the physical end, and after a week in which those two things moved apart, they are now moving together again. The physical picture is the worst of the conflict. Kpler has exports through the Strait of Hormuz averaging 8.5 million barrels a day in the week to October 12, close to 40% below pre-war normal, with daily flows swinging between 3 and 6 million barrels. UKMTO counts more than twenty vessels attacked this month across Hormuz, the Persian Gulf and the Gulf of Oman, and nine in or near the strait itself since October 12. Two cargo ships sank this week - only the second and third to sink in the region since the war began in February, on IMO data - with Iran's Revolutionary Guard claiming a mine sank one and the maritime security firm Marisks saying there is no independent evidence for that. An Iranian army chief says the strait will stay shut until Iran's rights are respected, while the US military disputes that Iran controls or has closed it, and tanker rates are at record highs. Iran's escalation is a response to the US naval blockade reinstated in July: having lost its own exports, Tehran's answer is to stop anyone else's. What changed this week is that the price finally followed. WTI has gone 90.409, 92.715, 94.810, 94.022 and now 97.290 across five readings of this file, up 5.92% on the week, with Brent at 105.311 and a six-day winning streak halting today at minus 0.69%. That matters because the price leg is what the FX board trades, and its resumption is the single reason the Canadian dollar was raised from 0 to plus 1: Macklem's Halifax threshold - if oil stays near 100 dollars the Bank would expect inflation to edge up, and there is a risk in delaying a hike too long - is effectively met, and the October 28 Monetary Policy Report has to replace a baseline assuming crude falls toward 75 dollars by mid-2027. The bear case has not gone away but it is policy-driven and dated rather than physical, which is exactly why USOIL is a low-conviction long: Washington is reported to be considering letting legal oil move on previously sanctioned tankers, which would cut freight costs; Trump has ruled out a nuclear strike on Iran; the IEA and G7 emergency release programme is a scheduled multi-month supply response; and the IEA's October report has supply rebounding in 2027 to tip the market into surplus, with diesel the tightest part at refining margins near 85 dollars after combined Middle East and Russian export losses of 1.3 million barrels a day in September. The FX read runs in three directions and one of them changed today. Oil-up is Canadian-dollar-positive and that channel is now live, which is the upgrade. Oil-up is also an inflation shock for the energy importers, and that is where the second front sits: European gas is the leg that keeps GER40 capped, with German gas at 86.73 EUR/MWh and plus 8.65% on the week against EU gas at 85.50 and plus 5.00%, both up more than 185% on the year - a direct margin headwind for an export-heavy industrial index. The UK series is the exception and it cuts the other way: UK gas at 211.47 GBp/thm, minus 1.02% today with its weekly gain halved to 4.40% from 9.88%, is precisely the data that undercuts the energy conditional Bailey softened in Bangkok on Friday, which is why sterling sits at plus 1 rather than plus 2. The third direction is the haven channel and it is the one that reversed. For five weeks war headlines produced no franc bid, then on Thursday a credit scare did, and this file upgraded the franc on it. Overnight the credit scare unwound and the franc gave the move back against six of seven majors while gold held 4,175.09. That is the distinction to keep: an oil shock is an inflation shock and is bearish a currency whose central bank sits at zero and cannot respond, a credit shock is a demand shock and is bullish it, and gold is bid by both because it trades US real rates as well as fear. The franc owns one leg; gold owns two. The war is still the reason oil is at 97 and therefore the reason the Canadian dollar was upgraded, but it has still never been the reason the franc rallies.",
